  "reason": "R6 is a particularly strong antecedent: in a closely related refractory BCC alloy, increasing Cr causes precipitation of Cr2Ta Laves phase with a network distribution in dendritic regions. This closely matches both the later intervention and its reticulated Cr-rich multicomponent Laves mediator. R2 independently confirms that Cr-containing refractory BCC alloys readily form Cr2Nb-type Laves phases, although there they reduce plasticity. R3 and several precipitation/composite studies establish that phase topology and continuity can determine whether a hard second phase merely embrittles the matrix or permits a useful strength–ductility balance. Other records support solid-solution, dislocation-density, and second-phase strengthening in refractory or related HEAs. Thus, although the precise 4.2 at.% Cr level and the beneficial crack-hindering action in HfMoTaTiZr would still require experimentation, an expert could plausibly propose Cr alloying to create a networked Laves phase and combine matrix hardening with topology-mediated crack control."
